>> The lesson of how to read the source code
>> and the spoilers
>> and then do analysis on it to build tables
>
> Yeah, I've been constructing a table of weapons that
> lists them by skill (so I can try to pick one I can reach
> expert in for a given role) that shows the damage and
> to-hit-bonus numbers,

There's an expanded weapons spoiler somewhere that also displays if a
weapon type stacks and if it is possible to multifire with it.  Because
of the value of missile combat over melee combat that's an extremely
valuable addition.

One thing I do is look up the skill limits in advance and write up a
skill slot strategy for any class I play often.  I decide what classes I
will ever want to advance, to what level and a very rough order.  Of
course survival always takes precedence so I do enhance skills not
according to the plan if there's a pressing need but having such a plan
in the first place keeps me from wasting limited skill slots on classes
with little long term benefit.  These write ups aren't in tabluar form
but they do draw on the analysis skills I learned doing tables about
prayer and gems.

> from which I picked up among
> other things that a katana is always worth trading up
> to from a regular long sword (of equal enchantment).

Katanas are very rare except in a samurai's initial equipment so many
times the first one I see is Snickersnee.  It's one of the artifacts
whose damage bonus applies to all monsters so it's a very good weapons.

> I've included artifacts, but a surprisingly large number
> of them aren't significantly better than the plain,
> non-artifact variety.

Junk artifacts.  Check out the prices that shops charge per artifact to
get a rough idea of their relative value.  But consider - Getting a junk
artifact as a gift still opens that skill slot and the gift artifact is
always rustproof.  Even junk artifacts beat their non-artifact versions.
Not that that means much for a war hammer ;^)

> Excalibur looks *significantly*
> better than I would have expected, particularly for
> an item that any character of the proper alignment
> can trivially create by dipping.  I may have to play
> some games with a lawful alignment just to try it out.

It's one of the best artifacts in the game because of it's non-damage
effects but it also beats any of the specialist artifacts becauses its
damage bonus applies to all monsters.  It beats Snickersnee.

>> Glass gems make acceptable missiles.
>> They do rock damage at 1/10th the weight.
>
> I've been carrying gems when I play as a caveman, yeah.
> But that's not a role I've had much success with yet.

Sling as not a commonly used weapon so gem missiles are not widely used.
I tend to consider it a good way to get rid of excess glass.

>> Some items I shuffle down some I don't.
>
> Right, that part matches in general with my practice
> (although "down" isn't necessarily always down; I
> tend to keep my main stash on the lowest level in
> the main dungeon with a known co-aligned altar, if
> I've found one; that can occasionally be up rather
> than down).

Down in the mines is actually up.  I also keep a main stash at the
nearest altar level and I'll shuttle far more items to my main stash.  I
still keep a per-level stash of any items that I found on that level
that weren't worth shuttling down plus any items that crossed an
invisible threshold of no longer being worth shuttling down because I
found something better.  I end up with a ton of plate/splint/banded
armor on the level with the mine entrance in some games and even more in
mine town in most games becuase those items are so heavy.


